1.35.0: red, green and blue

--red, --green, --blue: more or less of that color of light, in stops
(-2..2; +1 doubles it). Sliders "Red", "Green", "Blue" on the page,
beside Warm / cool and Tint.

They add no freedom, and are there on purpose. A picture's color has
three global numbers, and polajuice already had them as --ev, --temp
and --tint. But a beginner thinks "too much red", not "half a stop
toward magenta", and three numbers read off a gray patch are corrected
most directly channel by channel. So the same three degrees of freedom
are now also offered under the names everyone knows:

    red   =  ev + temp/2 + tint/4       ev   = (red + green + blue) / 3
    green =  ev          - tint/2       temp =  red - blue
    blue  =  ev - temp/2 + tint/4       tint = (red + blue - 2 green) * 2/3

The two sets add in the exponent of one set of channel gains, applied
where white balance always was, so they can be mixed freely. Tested:
all three up a stop is --ev 1 to the pixel; red up half and blue down
half is --temp 1 to the pixel; the general case agrees to one level's
rounding. Unlike the --filter identity this one has no conditions: all
six sit before the tone controls.

The tone equalizer's bands are placed "after exposure"; that now means
after the light as a whole. A gray's luminance moves with the channel
gains (and, slightly, with --temp and --tint, which was not accounted
for in 1.34), so the handles on the page's tone curve are placed by the
exposure, the raw stop and the luminance of the light's color together.
Renders are unaffected; only where the handles are drawn.

Scene keys red, green, blue: recipes, superjuice, serve, wasm,
super8ify (RED, GREEN, BLUE), completions, exported LUTs and darktable
styles. At zero, output is byte-identical to 1.34.0; a recipe from
before has no such keys and reads as zero.

Docs. The guide's white balance section gains "The same thing by
color": the three controls, an example of each, a table of casts and
their cures by opposite colors, and a plain statement that these are
--ev, --temp and --tint by another name, with when to prefer which;
chapter 2, the working-by-numbers table, the equivalence table and the
option tables are updated. README: the scene section, annex A (identity
1a, both directions) and annex D (twelve scene controls, still eight
dimensions). --help, the three man pages, RECIPES.md.

Tests: core (each channel doubles alone, the identity on pixels, keys,
the tone handles' shift under a channel gain); CLI (each acts, the two
exact identities, the general one, recipe replay, ranges, --filter red
still a filter); serve; wasm; page.
